@flexiarg meta/injury-routes-to-self-heal
@title Injury Routes META Selection to Self-Heal Algorithms
@sigils [👹/失]
@keywords META, injury, self-heal, algorithm, interoception, policy support, active inference
@audience War Machine designers, runtime maintainers, operators
@tone safety-operational
@references [meta/observe-the-meta-field meta/fill-meta-policy-slots meta/baldwin-ratchet-defeats-darkroom]
@status [status[ready] "evidence[operator example 2026-10-02: injured machine should leave out M/E/T and select a suitable A item]"]

! conclusion:
  When retained interoceptive evidence says ordinary work is unsafe or
  impossible, construct the live outer-policy family from applicable A/self-
  heal fillers and leave M/E/T instances out with the injury evidence attached.

  + context: The observed META field contains a typed injury whose repair has
    an approved algorithm and whose persistence would invalidate ordinary work.

  + IF:
    A tripwire, debugger stop, capability loss, or coherence failure identifies
    the injured capability and an approved A item declares that capability as
    its repair target.

  + HOWEVER:
    Merely adding a large penalty to M/E/T leaves unsafe acts in support and
    can still select them under noise. Conversely, treating every failure as
    injury creates a self-repair dark room that never returns to useful work.

  + THEN:
    Guard the self-heal arm by exact injury-to-algorithm capability matching.
    Admit matching A fillers; exclude M/E/T fillers with reason
    `:machine-injury-active`. State the re-arm observation that will restore
    ordinary support after the repair is verified.

  + BECAUSE:
    Policy support should express what the current organism can enact. A typed,
    reversible support change is clearer than a magic score and makes healing a
    first-class policy rather than an exception outside the AIF loop.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[After the A policy acts, observe the declared re-arm condition before restoring M/E/T support.]
